No, probably not. This looks like a connection leak, and a bigger pool would only delay the point where it fills up.

`withClient` in `db.js` releases the client only when `fn` succeeds:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, for example on a failed query, `client.release()` never runs and that connection stays checked out for good. Each error permanently removes one connection from the pool's 20.

`pool.log` matches that pattern. The pool sits at `total=20 idle=0` the whole time, and `waiting` keeps climbing (7, 11, 14, 19, 23, 28) instead of going up and down with load. Once all 20 are stuck, new requests wait until they hit `timeout exceeded when trying to connect`.

Real load at the limit would show `idle` sometimes going above 0 and `waiting` going down between bursts. Neither happens here. Errors from code that calls `withClient` would confirm the leak. Callers that use `pool.connect()` directly without releasing the client would cause the same thing.

No, raising the pool size would only delay the next exhaustion. The problem is probably a connection leak in `withClient` (`db.js:11-16`):

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws or rejects, `client.release()` never runs. That client stays checked out for the life of the process. Each failed query permanently takes one of the 20 slots, and once all 20 are gone, every later request waits until it hits the 2-second `connectionTimeoutMillis`.

`pool.log` fits that pattern. `idle=0` holds for the whole window and `waiting` climbs steadily from 7 to 28, with no recovery. That is a pool whose connections are not coming back, not one that is briefly overloaded. With `max: 40` you would get the same curve, just later.

A leak isn't fully proven, because seven log lines can't separate leaked clients from very slow queries. Look at `pg_stat_activity` for this app's connections. Sessions that sit `idle in transaction` or `idle` for a long time while the pool reports them checked out confirm the leak. Long-running `active` queries would point to slow queries instead.

